ter.\n\n### `add_op` / `multiply_op` / `subtract_op` — C 51-61 / Rust 116-128 — EQUIVALENT\ngcc at the build's flags (`-fPIC`, no `-O`) emits plain `addl`/`imull`/`subl`, i.e. two's-complement wraparound; `wrapping_add/mul/sub` match. Verified over 400k random pairs plus the edge grid (`INT_MAX+1`, `INT_MIN-1`, `INT_MAX*INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN*-1`, …). The ignored `unused1/unused2` args are ignored identically (I passed garbage in them).\n\n### `divide_op` — C 63-66 / Rust 131-136\n- `b == 0` guard before the division, returns 0: **EQUIVALENT** (order preserved).\n- Truncation toward zero for mixed signs: **EQUIVALENT** — exhaustive `a,b ∈ [-40,40]` plus 400k random pairs.\n- **DIVERGENCE (C-UB).** `divide_op(INT_MIN, -1, 0, 0)`: C is killed by **SIGFPE (signal 8)** — measured in a forked child — because `idivl` overflows. Rust returns **-2147483648** (`wrapping_div`). Unreachable from `inreftree`: `op_string` is `\"+*-%\"`, which contains no `/`, so `OP_DIVIDE` is never selected.\n\n### `modulo_op` — C 68-71 / Rust 139-144\n- `b == 0` guard: **EQUIVALENT**.\n- Remainder takes the sign of the dividend (`-7 % 3 == -1`, `7 % -3 == 1`): **EQUIVALENT** — exhaustive `[-40,40]²`.\n- **DIVERGENCE (C-UB).** `modulo_op(INT_MIN, -1, 0, 0)`: C → **SIGFPE (signal 8)**; Rust returns **0**. Unreachable from `inreftree`: the `%` path always passes `b = target_id ∈ {1,2}`.\n\n### `find_node_by_id` — C 73-80 / Rust 151-165\n- Loop bound / first-match / `&node_table[i]` / `NULL` fallthrough: **EQUIVALENT**.\n- **Rust caches `node_count` into `count` (line 154) where C re-reads it each iteration (line 74) — EQUIVALENT.** Nothing in the loop body can mutate `node_count` (no calls at all), so the cache is unobservable single-threaded. gcc itself caches it at `-O1`+.\n- Negative `node_count`: loop body never runs in both. **EQUIVALENT**.\n- **DIVERGENCE (C-UB, layout-driven).** For `node_count > 50` the C `.so` places `node_count` **immediately after** `node_table` (offset +2600, `0x4060` → `0x4a88`), so `node_table[50].id` *aliases `node_count` itself*. The Rust `.so` places `node_count` **4 bytes before** `node_table` (`0x507e4` vs `0x507e8`). Concrete distinguishing input: zero `node_table`, set `node_count = 51`, call `find_node_by_id(51)` → **C returns `&node_table[50]`; Rust returns NULL.** Same for 52→52 and 53→53. Requires writing the exported global out of range; unreachable via the API since `add_tree_node` refuses at `>= 50`.\n\n### `add_tree_node` — C 82-111 / Rust 168-205 — EQUIVALENT\nEvery clause checked and byte-verified:\n- Capacity check `node_count >= 50` as a **signed** `int` compare: matches (`MAX_NODES as c_int`).\n- Field write order, and the fact that `left_child_id`/`right_child_id` are set to `-1` *before* the parent lookup: matches.\n- **`c_strncpy` is a faithful `strncpy`**: copies at most n, stops after copying the NUL, then zero-pads the remainder to exactly n=31 bytes. Verified byte-exact for source lengths 0..39 against a `0xAA`-prefilled table — including the no-NUL case (len ≥ 31, where `strncpy` leaves no terminator and only `label[31] = '\\0'` (C 94 / Rust 187) saves it).\n- `find_node_by_id(parent_id)` runs **before** `node_count++`, so the row being written is invisible to the lookup: matches (this is why `add_tree_node(id, v, id, ...)` can't self-parent on the first insert).\n- The `parent == NULL || parent->id != parent_id` short-circuit order (C 98 / Rust 191) is preserved; the redundant second test is kept.\n- Early `return -1` leaves the row's 52 bytes **already written** and `node_count` un-incremented: byte-identical in both.\n- Left-then-right child slot fill, silently dropping a 3rd child: matches.\n- `node_count++; return node_count - 1;` (C 109-110) vs `node_count = node_count.wrapping_add(1); node_count.wrapping_sub(1)` (Rust 202-203) — both re-read the global after the store. Matches.\n- Verified over 60,000 random insert sequences with arbitrary/duplicate/negative ids and parents (return value + full table bytes + `node_count` compared after every call), 20,000 acyclic trees of 1..55 nodes, and the capacity boundary `node_count ∈ 0..52`.\n- `label == NULL`: both fault. Not a comparable value.\n- `node_count < 0`: both write `node_table[negative]`. Because of the reversed global order the *clobbered* bytes differ (in Rust the row overlaps `node_count`). Measured return value happens to match (`-1`) in both. Deep UB; unreachable from `inreftree` (which sets `node_count = 0` first).\n\n### `calculate_tree_sum` — C 113-131 / Rust 208-228 — EQUIVALENT\n- `node == NULL || node->id != node_id` order and the redundant recheck: preserved.\n- `!= -1` sentinel tests (so a child id of **0** *is* followed, and a child id of `-2` *is* followed and returns 0): preserved.\n- Left recursion strictly before right; `sum` accumulates with wraparound: preserved. `node->right_child_id` is re-read through the pointer after the left recursion in both.\n- Diamond double-counting (a node reachable via two parents is added twice) reproduced.\n- Cycles → unbounded recursion. Measured: **both die with SIGSEGV (signal 11)** — identical failure. *Doc note:* `ERRORS.md` row U5 claims both die with `fatal runtime error: stack overflow` / signal 6; the actual measurement is signal 11 for both. The behavior matches; only that doc line is wrong.\n\n### `parse_operation` — C 133-150 / Rust 235-254 — EQUIVALENT\n- `op_str == NULL ||` is the **first** operand in both, so NULL returns `OP_ADD` without any deref. Order preserved.\n- Precedence `+` > `*` > `-` > `/` > `%`, and the `OP_ADD` fallthrough: preserved. (So `\"%-*+\"` → `OP_ADD`, `\"%/\"` → `OP_DIVIDE`.)\n- **`c_strchr` vs libc `strchr`**: it tests `ch == c` *before* `ch == 0`, so it matches glibc even for a NUL needle (returns the terminator) — and for the five non-NUL needles actually used it is exactly `strchr`. The `*p as u8` reinterpretation of signed `c_char` is correct for all 256 byte values.\n- Exhaustively verified: **all 255 one-byte strings and all 65,025 two-byte strings**, plus `NULL`, the four labels, `\"+*-%\"`, high-bit bytes (`0x80`, `0xFF`) and a 42-char string. 0 mismatches.\n- C returns `Operation` (gcc widens to `unsigned int`), Rust returns `c_int` — same 32-bit value for 1..5.\n\n### `get_operation_func` — C 152-161 / Rust 257-266 — EQUIVALENT\n- `switch ((int)op)` 1..5 with `default: add_op` matches the Rust `match` with `_ => Some(add_op)`. Verified for `op ∈ [-5,10]`, and for `0xFFFFFFFF` (C's `(int)op` → -1 → default; Rust `_`) — same.\n- **Pointer identity also matches**: for all of 1..5 the returned pointer equals that library's own `dlsym` address for the corresponding symbol, and 0/-1/99 all return the library's own `add_op`. So even a test that compares raw function pointers passes.\n\n### `inreftree` — C 163-197 / Rust 293-338 — EQUIVALENT\nTraced fully: 4 nodes, ids 1..4, `node_table[1].left_child_id = 4`; the label scan skips `\"root\"` (no `'l'`) and matches `\"left\"` at index 1, so `target_id = 2`, then `target_id = 1` iff `param2 == 0`; `tree_sum = param1+param2+param3+param4` (wrapping).\n- `node_count = 0` reset, then 4 inserts with byte-identical literal labels: **EQUIVALENT**.\n- Loop `break` at the *first* `'l'`-containing label: **EQUIVALENT**. Rust caching `node_count` into `count` (line 304) is unobservable — nothing in the loop mutates it.\n- `target == NULL || target->value == 0` operand order (C 180 / Rust 317): preserved.\n- `tree_sum % 4` truncates toward zero in both (C `%`, Rust `wrapping_rem`), giving `-3..3`; `INT_MIN % 4 == 0` in both: **EQUIVALENT**.\n- **The negative-index OOB read of `op_string` (C 187) — EQUIVALENT for the reference build, and I verified the hard-coded bytes rather than trusting the comment.** `objdump -s -j .rodata` on the actual `c_src/build/libtranslated_rust.so` shows a 24-byte jump table at `0x2000` followed by `\"root\\0left\\0right\\0left-left\\0+*-%\\0\"`, putting `\"+*-%\"` at `0x2032`. Therefore `op_string[-1] == 0x00`, `[-2] == 0x74 ('t')`, `[-3] == 0x66 ('f')` — exactly what Rust's `RODATA` (line 289) at `OP_STRING_OFFSET = 26` yields (indices 25/24/23). Full mapping matches: `0→'+'→ADD, 1→'*'→MUL, 2→'-'→SUB, 3→'%'→MOD, -1→'\\0'→ADD, -2→'t'→ADD, -3→'f'→ADD`.\n  - *Fragility note (not a divergence):* the hard-coded `RODATA` is build-specific. At `-O1/-O2/-Os` gcc tail-merges `\"left\"` into `\"left-left\"`, moving `\"+*-%\"` to offset 21; clang `-O2` puts `\"+*-%\"` at `.rodata` offset 0 so the reads go before the section entirely. I rebuilt the C at `-O1` and with `clang -O2` and re-ran the full 2.19M-case `inreftree` sweep against the Rust: **0 mismatches in both**, because the preceding bytes are still non-operators in every layout. So the hazard is latent, not live.\n- `int op_value = (int)op;` (C 190) and `let _op_value = op;` (Rust 330) are both dead with no side effects: **EQUIVALENT**.\n- `func.unwrap()` cannot panic — `get_operation_func` always returns `Some`.\n- Verified over the **exhaustive 28⁴ = 614,656 grid** of `{0,±1..±8,±100,INT_MAX,INT_MIN,INT_MAX-1,INT_MIN+1,±2³⁰,±1000000007}` plus 300,000 random quadruples, comparing the return value **and** the full 2600-byte `node_table` **and** `node_count` after every call. 2,194,323 checks, 0 mismatches. Repeated against the debug build: 0 mismatches (no `wrapping_*` gaps that would panic in debug).\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| # | Function | Status | Distinguishing input |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 | `divide_op` (C 65 / Rust 135) | DIVERGENCE (C-UB) | `divide_op(INT_MIN, -1, 0, 0)`: C → SIGFPE; Rust → `-2147483648` |\n| D2 | `modulo_op` (C 70 / Rust 143) | DIVERGENCE (C-UB) | `modulo_op(INT_MIN, -1, 0, 0)`: C → SIGFPE; Rust → `0` |\n| D3 | `find_node_by_id` (C 74-76 / Rust 156-160) | DIVERGENCE (C-UB, global ordering) | zeroed table, `node_count = 51`, `find_node_by_id(51)`: C → `&node_table[50]` (aliases `node_count`); Rust → `NULL`. C lays out `node_count` at `node_table+2600`; Rust at `node_table-4`. |\n\nAll 13 items (11 functions + 2 globals) are otherwise EQUIVALENT, including every off-by-one, short-circuit ordering, `strncpy` zero-padding, remainder sign, global re-read/caching, and the deliberately reproduced `op_string` out-of-bounds read. None of D1-D3 is reachable from `inreftree`, and each requires an input that is already undefined behavior in the C.","usage":{"total_tokens":67183,"tool_uses":22,"duration_ms":530323},"uuid":"8f22ee3a-07f2-4251-91c8-a193a57f5489","session_id":"e1c21d0a-4bcf-4f52-9a28-e367512a5538"}
