{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_f52tt3f7emehhpshe3cv2n73zm27fnbqd4nbzxegnq6e5pmo2eaa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01AVwbnnnYZtatGG9sKUZt8X","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-Y9FE6L/translated_rust/ERRORS.md","old_string":"| U4 | `find_node_by_id` / `calculate_tree_sum` | `node_count > 50` | reads `node_table[50..node_count]`, past the 2600-byte object | Out-of-bounds read; unreachable from `inreftree`. Row 7 covers the *reachable* half of this (`add_tree_node`, which rejects before indexing). |","new_string":"| U4 | `find_node_by_id` / `calculate_tree_sum` | `node_count > 50` | reads `node_table[50..node_count]`, past the 2600-byte object | Out-of-bounds read; unreachable from `inreftree`. Row 7 covers the *reachable* half of this (`add_tree_node`, which rejects before indexing). **This is a measured C-vs-Rust divergence, caused by the linker's arbitrary BSS ordering rather than by the translation:** `nm -D -S` shows the C `.so` at `node_table @0x4060` (2600 bytes) and `node_count @0x4a88` — i.e. `node_count` sits *exactly* at `node_table + 2600`, so the out-of-bounds row `node_table[50]` **aliases `node_count` itself**. The Rust `.so` puts `node_count @0x507e4`, 4 bytes *before* `node_table @0x507e8`. Distinguishing input: zeroed table, `node_count = 51`, `find_node_by_id(51)` → C returns `&node_table[50]` (reading its own `node_count`), Rust returns `NULL`. Reaching this requires the caller to write the exported `node_count` past its documented range, which is already UB in C; the ordering of two independent BSS objects is not something a translation can (or portably should) reproduce. |"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":904,"cache_read_input_tokens":208620,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":904,"ephemeral_1h